{"name":"Falling Star","hex":"#cad5c8","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"falling star","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A meteor."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/falling_star"}],"entries":[{"title":"Meteor","blocks":[{"text":"A meteor, known colloquially as a shooting star, is a glowing streak of a small body going through Earth's atmosphere, after being heated to incandescence by collisions with air molecules in the upper atmosphere, creating a streak of light via its rapid motion and sometimes also by shedding glowing material in its wake. Meteors typically occur in the mesosphere at altitudes from 76–100 kilometres. The root word meteor comes from the Greek μετεωρίτης (meteōrítēs), meaning \"high in the air\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Cosmic_Fireball_Falling_Over_ALMA.jpg/330px-Cosmic_Fireball_Falling_Over_ALMA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meteor"},{"title":"The Falling Star","blocks":[{"text":"The Falling Star is a 1950 West German drama film directed by Harald Braun and starring Werner Krauss, Dieter Borsche and Gisela Uhlen. It was entered into the 1951 Cannes Film Festival. It was shot at the Bavaria Studios in Munich. The film's sets were designed by the art directors Hans Sohnle and Fritz Lück."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/9/94/The_Falling_Star.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Falling_Star"},{"title":"Falling Star (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Falling Star is a 2014 Spanish historical film directed by Lluís Miñarro which stars Alex Brendemühl as King Amadeo. It features dialogue in Catalan and Spanish."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Falling_Star_(film)"},{"title":"Star","blocks":[{"text":"A star is a luminous spheroid of plasma held together by self-gravity. The nearest star to Earth is the Sun. Many other stars are visible to the naked eye at night; their immense distances from Earth make them appear as fixed points of light. The most prominent stars have been categorised into constellations and asterisms, and many of the brightest stars have proper names. Astronomers have assembled star catalogues that identify the known stars and provide standardized stellar designations. The observable universe contains an estimated 1022 to 1024 stars. Only about 4,000 of these stars are visible to the naked eye—all within the Milky Way galaxy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/The_Sun_in_white_light.jpg/330px-The_Sun_in_white_light.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Star"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/P10220XX.01.49.MESZ.14.08.2019.Berlin.Perseiden.x1.0.gif/500px-P10220XX.01.49.MESZ.14.08.2019.Berlin.Perseiden.x1.0.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/P10220XX.01.49.MESZ.14.08.2019.Berlin.Perseiden.x1.0.gif/500px-P10220XX.01.49.MESZ.14.08.2019.Berlin.Perseiden.x1.0.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A meteoroid of the Perseids with a size of about 1 cm (3⁄8 in) entering the earth's atmosphere in real time. The meteoroid is at the bright head of the trail, and the ionisation of the mesosphere is still visible in the tail.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AP10220XX.01.49.MESZ.14.08.2019.Berlin.Perseiden.x1.0.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Leonid_Meteor.jpg/500px-Leonid_Meteor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Leonid_Meteor.jpg/1280px-Leonid_Meteor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Leonid_Meteor.jpg/500px-Leonid_Meteor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A meteor of the Leonid meteor shower; the photograph shows the meteor, afterglow, and wake as distinct components","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALeonid_Meteor.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Meteor_burst.jpg/500px-Meteor_burst.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Meteor_burst.jpg/1280px-Meteor_burst.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Meteor_burst.jpg/500px-Meteor_burst.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Multiple meteors photographed over an extended exposure time during a meteor shower","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMeteor_burst.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/PSM_V18_D201_Shower_of_perseids_sept_6_and_7.jpg/500px-PSM_V18_D201_Shower_of_perseids_sept_6_and_7.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/PSM_V18_D201_Shower_of_perseids_sept_6_and_7.jpg/1280px-PSM_V18_D201_Shower_of_perseids_sept_6_and_7.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/PSM_V18_D201_Shower_of_perseids_sept_6_and_7.jpg/500px-PSM_V18_D201_Shower_of_perseids_sept_6_and_7.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Meteor shower on chart","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APSM_V18_D201_Shower_of_perseids_sept_6_and_7.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/common